  of endless stories of a new crisis of  trust  um that we're always just in some sort  of crisis state where it's  trust is collapsing in individuals or  institutions  and the truth is when you look at the  long-term trends there have been  some declines in some countries  but it's a very stable pattern or even  rising pattern of trust in  in some institutions in some context so  uh we are probably over exaggerating  this sense of crisis of trust and you  can see that  when you ask questions about people of  do you think  trust is collapsing we're much more  likely to think  trust is collapsing than it actually is  in practice so  uh 64 of people say that  people trust politicians less today  than they did 30 years ago only 10  percent say they trust them  more but when you look at the trends we  were actually just as  untrusting back in 30 years three  decades ago  as we are now so we've got a sense of  change but it's not really changing as  much as people think  you mentioned trust and policy can you  tell us who  are the most trusted and the least  trusted groups in our society  well uh good news from uh from the  situation that we're in now that  actually the most trusted  group in a 22 country study uh that was  done  uh earlier this year is scientists so  60 percent of people across this 22  countries study say they trust  scientists  and then it's doctors next so we've got  this very  strong sense of trust in scientific  information and medical  practice as well teachers come next and  then  at the other end of the spectrum you  have politicians with  in this 22 country study just nine  percent of the public  trusting uh politicians compared with 60  percent for professions like uh  scientists and  and then when you look at institutions  it tends to be the police and judiciary  that come  uh top and at the the other end of the  spectrum on institutions it is things  like political parties  and national government one really  important point though  is it matters hugely which country  you're looking at  so both in terms of level of trust and  the direction that trust is going in so  in norway 62 percent of people  trust their national parliament and  that's only 11  in slovenia it's rising in switzerland  but it's falling in spain and on  scientists barely anyone has low levels  of trust in spain  but over 40 percent of the population in  bosnia  say they have low trust in spain in  scientists so  it is it is a point of being very  specific there's  not one global trend or global measure  of trust it really depends  on which country you're looking at and  which profession  or institution you're looking at so so  what can  science and scientific institutions do  in order  to increa increase their trustworthiness  i think we've got a really good base as  we can see from the trust measures  around the world we are doing pretty  well and then  during the pandemic it trust is held up  in science so there's one 14-country  study that showed  that there's been a rise in trust in  both science and scientists  and a decline in science skepticism  people rejecting  science since the crisis began and  we can see that across all sorts of  different  countries in terms of how do you  improve uh levels of trust it's really  about transparency and communication  being  open and honest with people there's a  there's an interesting stat  stats in the in the uk that the most  visited website  ever in the history of bbc news  websites is people looking for  information about the  the rate of infection in their own area  people are interested in this  and and what the evidence suggests is  more communication  and particularly neutral communication  not aggressive communication with people  open honest transparent with people  we can learn more during the berlin  science week can you tell us  what can we expect from your session  well we will be looking at trust trends  in trust  uh what the future looks like on trust  uh  given that it's 